ocratic party's  compromises end up looking like a  surrender  yes nothing would give me more pleasure  than to tank a billionaire dark money  fossil fuel exxon lobbyist drafted  energy infrastructure bill if they come  after our child care and climate  priorities  who better to talk to about all of this  than a founding member of the  progressive squad earlier today i spoke  with massachusetts congresswoman ayanna  pressley about the democrats tightening  window for legislation and the simmering  tensions between the parties centrists  and the progressives  congresswoman ayanna presley of  massachusetts thank you so much for  joining us on the show tonight let's  talk about one issue that this major  reconciliation bill puts front and  center climate change just yesterday you  published an op-ed in the boston globe  saying that investments in climate  infrastructure cannot wait republicans  and some of your democratic colleagues  disagree they say it's pork it's a  distraction what do you say to them  climate is infrastructure again we know  that we need to make those investments  in traditional forms of infrastructure  but you know infrastructure is more than  physical it is human as well and this is  a matter of life and death we know that  climate change is an existential threat  to our planet and certainly the most  vulnerable low-income communities of  color have been disproportionately  impacted by climate higher rates of  asthma more vulnerable to sea level rise  to extreme weather um  in equitable tree canopies my district  the massachusetts 7th congressional  district was the hardest hit in our  congressional delegation in the  commonwealth and if you line up those uh  highest  asthma rate with the highest rates of  profit infection hospitalization and  death with our heat maps they sync up  perfectly so we have got to make these  robust investments and um three things  that i'm calling for is a civilian  conservation corps um a transition to  renewable energy and a divestment from  fossil fuels so this is a  a matter of life and death and it is a  burden  bore by communities of color given  proximity to  highways toxic waste jobs to  transportation deserts food apartheid  systems unequal access to health care  again highest rates of asthma so this is  also a racial justice issue  it is indeed although one of the big  obstacles to the reconciliation bill's  passage especially the climate stuff in  it is democratic senator joe manchin of  west virginia who also just happens to  chair the senate energy and natural  resources committee how do you explain  to your voters and to climate activists  and to young people that the democratic  lawmaker writing the legislation on  climate change in the senate made his  fortune as a coal broker and enjoys the  backing of big coal and big oil how is  that not a conflict of interest  well it certainly is and i think senator  manchin owes the people of west virginia  an explanation because he is not  prioritizing  the american people  we have got to make these bold  investments 3.5 children was already a  compromise and this is simply the cost  of making up for long overdue uh  divestment and under investment in our  communities and the costs are covered  you know if we stay the course and we  tax the richest of the rich it's a  simple solution  but um you know joe manchin isn't in  charge thank god the people are and i'm  going to continue to fight for them that  we advance a bold bill with robust  investments that meets the needs of the  american people and they are urgent as  they bear the weight of these um  convergent crises a public health uh  pandemic in crisis  a pandemic induced recession and again a  national reckoning on racial injustice  so care is infrastructure housing is  infrastructure  and climate is infrastructure  another obstacle to progressive spending  uh goals is arizona senator kistan  cinema whose staff and people around her  seemed to brief axios yesterday 